#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268872 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133841 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "90f7718f-e014-5232-acb1-6773ab79f61d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Double Integral Defined Over a Type II Region"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b299a6b-4484-532a-9f8c-c14f516cc562" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{8}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266245} {:answer/id #uuid "276d6c64-ff31-5537-9cfc-e67b94462ffd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{8}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266496} {:answer/id #uuid "625522d9-ae80-5048-aff1-7d51ffb91b4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{4}+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267009} {:answer/id #uuid "683122b3-f461-5f8c-9000-a8fdb77f528a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{4}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267067} {:answer/id #uuid "77f6ea70-051f-52ad-bfa3-1aafd5a5a61d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{8}+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267195}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b299a6b-4484-532a-9f8c-c14f516cc562" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{8}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266245} :answer-field/id #uuid "5098efbb-c619-55aa-9d7a-009fdc05844c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268550}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c2534dab-da9e-52d9-8510-a8b1311ad552" :question/math-academy-id "q-132529" :question/problem "Calculate ${∬}_{D}x{e}^{{y}^{3}}\\,dA,$ where $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,-y\\le x\\le 2y}.$" :question/worked-solution "The integral is defined over a type II region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,-y\\le x\\le 2y}.$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}x{e}^{{y}^{3}}\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-y}^{2y}x{e}^{{y}^{3}}\\,dx\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-y}^{2y}x{e}^{{y}^{3}}\\,dx\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{{y}^{3}}[{\\int }_{-y}^{2y}x\\,dx]dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{{y}^{3}}{[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}]}_{-y}^{2y}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{{y}^{3}}(2{y}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2})dy \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}3{y}^{2}{e}^{{y}^{3}}\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}3{y}^{2}{e}^{{y}^{3}}\\,dy & =\\frac{1}{2}{[{e}^{{y}^{3}}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{8}-{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{8}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134298 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133839 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2ccbe70d-24f4-5e9c-8536-9ccab99f571b" :knowledge-point/title "Representing a Double Integral as a Repeated Integral When the Limits are Not Given"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5bc9fc65-1739-5a7c-8405-faa5b206275d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{4/3}[{\\int }_{y/2}^{2-y}xy\\,dx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324729} {:answer/id #uuid "68b38b5d-aad2-529b-a2d8-79a2b6ddb413" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{4/3}[{\\int }_{2-y}^{y/2}xy\\,dx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324845} {:answer/id #uuid "a7d2cf74-7d71-5a5b-ac1a-00940077dba2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{y/2}[{\\int }_{0}^{2-y}xy\\,dx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325399} {:answer/id #uuid "ae4c1494-e680-57a5-bec4-f41594ee3b5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{2-y}^{y/2}[{\\int }_{0}^{4/3}xy\\,dx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325450} {:answer/id #uuid "f44c68bb-fac5-5af3-ac88-abac96d890b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{y/2}^{2-y}[{\\int }_{0}^{4/3}xy\\,dx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326121}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5bc9fc65-1739-5a7c-8405-faa5b206275d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{4/3}[{\\int }_{y/2}^{2-y}xy\\,dx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324729} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d4129ae-aa8f-5600-8743-4d0691281050" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326448}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "89052f85-16e5-5038-8862-a854c23cc74d" :question/math-academy-id "q-40596" :question/problem "![](images/c7/c752eb32fc94ae630ef3998c81a6b9b0af5c222a367f87db8bbacb877716c16c.png)\n\nIf $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the lines $x=2-y,\\,$ $x=\\frac{y}{2},$ and $y=0,$ as shown above, then ${∬}_{D}xy\\,dA=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's represent $D$ as a type II region. \n\n- To determine the $y$-limits, we need to find the $y$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-y & =\\frac{y}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2}y & =-2 \\\\ y & =\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $y$-limits are \n$0\\le y\\le \\frac{4}{3}.$\n\n- To determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](images/e6/e6c9332f8f59e2561af9ba5aa89311263fb772dc987ed0b15d91a6d5eea5cf8e.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{4}{3},\\,\\frac{y}{2}\\le x\\le 2-y}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}xy\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{4/3}{\\int }_{y/2}^{2-y}xy\\,dx\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4/3}[{\\int }_{y/2}^{2-y}xy\\,dx]dy\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
